          "reason": "A finite rate model consists of a finite nonempty state set $S$ and rates $q_{ij}\\ge0$ in seconds$^{-1}$ for $i\\ne j$. Boundary nonempty-choice: No choice principle is used; the finite data are fixed and all sums are finite. Item: def-td-detailed-balance-rate-model.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Definition",
            "source_excerpt": "A finite rate model consists of a finite nonempty state set $S$ and rates $q_{ij}\\ge0$ in seconds$^{-1}$ for $i\\ne j$. Set $q_{ii}=-\\sum_{j\\ne i}q_{ij}$. A row probability vector is $p=(p_i)_{i\\in S}$ with $p_i\\ge0$ and $\\sum_i p_i=1$; write $\\Delta(S)=\\{p\\in[0,1]^S:\\sum_i p_i=1\\}$. A time-dependent law is a coordinatewise continuously differentiable map $p:[0,\\infty)\\to\\Delta(S)$, with a right derivative at $t=0$, that satisfies the continuous-time master equation $\\dot p_i(t)=\\sum_{j\\ne i}[p_j(t)q_{ji}-p_i(t)q_{ij}]$ for every $t\\ge0$. A strictly positive normalized vector $\\pi$ is a detailed-balance law when $\\pi_iq_{ij}=\\pi_jq_{ji}$ for every distinct pair. Its undirected rate graph has an edge $\\{i,j\\}$ exactly when $\\pi_iq_{ij}>0$; detailed balance makes this equivalent to $\\pi_jq_{ji}>0$.\n\nOn a finite nonempty state set $S$, a discrete transition kernel is a matrix $K:S\\times S\\to[0,1]$ satisfying $\\sum_{j\\in S}K(i,j)=1$ for every $i\\in S$. For a strictly positive row probability vector $\\pi\\in\\Delta(S)$, the kernel satisfies $\\pi$-detailed balance when $\\pi_iK(i,j)=\\pi_jK(j,i)$ for every $i,j\\in S$. This identity pairs the supports of forward and reversed transitions. A rate model or a reversible kernel is a mathematical/physical model class; no observed trajectory is asserted by the definition.",
