        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Let $M$ be a finite memory-state set and $R$ a finite retained-register set. Boundary nonempty-choice: No choice principle is used; the finite data are fixed and all sums are finite. Item: def-td-finite-memory-reset-model.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Definition",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $M$ be a finite memory-state set and $R$ a finite retained-register set. A joint preparation is a probability law $p(m,r)\\ge0$ with $\\sum_{m,r}p(m,r)=1$. Write $a_r=\\sum_m p(m,r)$ and, for $a_r>0$, $q(m|r)=p(m,r)/a_r$. Use the finite Shannon entropy in nats and the conditional entropy $H(M|R)$ exactly as defined by [[lem-tdmath-conditional-memory-entropy]]. For a fixed target memory state $m_0$, a reset is the endpoint map $p'(m,r)=\\mathbf 1_{\\{m=m_0\\}}a_r$: it places the memory at $m_0$ while leaving the register marginal unchanged. The definition alone assigns no physical entropy, work cost, heat transfer, or implementation.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "2e5dfc0addbaddadc87c7e64efb136378abfee6c058ea6865c37b16bffd29f0d",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
