    "pthm-td-finite-pathwise-first-law": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-td-driven-finite-path-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Take $\\Omega$ to be a finite nonempty microstate set as in [[def-td-finite-microstate-model]]: distinct elements count as distinct microstates, and the physical energy values are finite and measured in joules in the declared rest frame. For the physical internal-energy interpretation, use one declared fixed Galilean chart for every stage, with the bulk center-of-mass and separately tracked external-energy sectors suppressed from this finite protocol. The companion mechanical model supplies the center-of-mass data required by the microstate supplier; when the common chart is called a center-of-mass rest chart throughout the protocol, its center-of-mass velocity is constant over the modeled interval, and one constant boost selects that chart. This fixed-chart idealization does not infer a rest chart from the finite set or transition kernels, and does not describe an accelerating bulk body. Fix an integer $m\\ge1$ and stage energy functions $E_k:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R\\,\\mathrm J$ for $k=0,\\ldots,m$, with the energy offsets fixed by the declared protocol model. Fix inverse temperature $\\beta>0$ in joules$^{-1}$. The finite partition sum and canonical probability vector at stage $k$ are $Z_k=\\sum_{i\\in\\Omega}e^{-\\beta E_k(i)}$ and $\\pi_k(i)=e^{-\\beta E_k(i)}/Z_k$. Since $\\Omega$ is nonempty and finite and each energy is finite, $0<Z_k<\\infty$ and $\\pi_k$ is a strictly positive probability vector.\n\nA transition kernel at stage $k$ is a matrix $K_k(i,j)\\ge0$ with $\\sum_{j\\in\\Omega}K_k(i,j)=1$. In the forward stage order, the energy changes instantaneously from $E_{k-1}$ to $E_k$ while the state is still $i_{k-1}$; the subsequent bath transition samples $i_k$ using $K_k(i_{k-1},i_k)$ at fixed energy $E_k$. A path is $\\omega=(i_0,\\ldots,i_m)\\in\\Omega^{m+1}$. The initial law is a probability vector $\\mu_0:\\Omega\\to[0,1]$ with $\\sum_{i\\in\\Omega}\\mu_0(i)=1$. Its path probability is $P_F(\\omega)=\\mu_0(i_0)\\prod_{k=1}^mK_k(i_{k-1},i_k)$; summing over all paths gives $1$ by the normalization of $\\mu_0$ and the row sums of the kernels.\n\nWork on the system is positive when energy is supplied to it, and heat into the system is positive when a bath transition raises its energy. Define $W(\\omega)=\\sum_{k=1}^m[E_k(i_{k-1})-E_{k-1}(i_{k-1})]$ and $Q_{\\mathrm{in}}(\\omega)=\\sum_{k=1}^m[E_k(i_k)-E_k(i_{k-1})]$, both in joules. Define $F_k=-\\beta^{-1}\\log Z_k$ and $\\Delta F=F_m-F_0=-\\beta^{-1}\\log(Z_m/Z_0)$. These are finite-model quantities; their physical interpretation is conditional on the adopted preparation and transition assumptions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Insert the intermediate energies and telescope: $E_m(i_m)-E_0(i_0)=\\sum_{k=1}^m[E_k(i_k)-E_{k-1}(i_{k-1})]$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For each $k$, split the summand as $E_k(i_{k-1})-E_{k-1}(i_{k-1})+E_k(i_k)-E_k(i_{k-1})$. Summing the first terms gives $W(\\omega)$ and the second terms give $Q_{\\mathrm{in}}(\\omega)$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement inherits a finite nonempty microstate set and a path with m≥1 from the model definition."
