    "pthm-td-markov-relative-entropy-and-convergence": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-td-detailed-balance-rate-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A finite rate model consists of a finite nonempty state set $S$ and rates $q_{ij}\\ge0$ in seconds$^{-1}$ for $i\\ne j$. Set $q_{ii}=-\\sum_{j\\ne i}q_{ij}$. A row probability vector is $p=(p_i)_{i\\in S}$ with $p_i\\ge0$ and $\\sum_i p_i=1$; write $\\Delta(S)=\\{p\\in[0,1]^S:\\sum_i p_i=1\\}$. A time-dependent law is a coordinatewise continuously differentiable map $p:[0,\\infty)\\to\\Delta(S)$, with a right derivative at $t=0$, that satisfies the continuous-time master equation $\\dot p_i(t)=\\sum_{j\\ne i}[p_j(t)q_{ji}-p_i(t)q_{ij}]$ for every $t\\ge0$. A strictly positive normalized vector $\\pi$ is a detailed-balance law when $\\pi_iq_{ij}=\\pi_jq_{ji}$ for every distinct pair. Its undirected rate graph has an edge $\\{i,j\\}$ exactly when $\\pi_iq_{ij}>0$; detailed balance makes this equivalent to $\\pi_jq_{ji}>0$.\n\nOn a finite nonempty state set $S$, a discrete transition kernel is a matrix $K:S\\times S\\to[0,1]$ satisfying $\\sum_{j\\in S}K(i,j)=1$ for every $i\\in S$. For a strictly positive row probability vector $\\pi\\in\\Delta(S)$, the kernel satisfies $\\pi$-detailed balance when $\\pi_iK(i,j)=\\pi_jK(j,i)$ for every $i,j\\in S$. This identity pairs the supports of forward and reversed transitions. A rate model or a reversible kernel is a mathematical/physical model class; no observed trajectory is asserted by the definition.\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-td-detailed-balance-rate-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A finite rate model consists of a finite nonempty state set $S$ and rates $q_{ij}\\ge0$ in seconds$^{-1}$ for $i\\ne j$. Set $q_{ii}=-\\sum_{j\\ne i}q_{ij}$. A row probability vector is $p=(p_i)_{i\\in S}$ with $p_i\\ge0$ and $\\sum_i p_i=1$; write $\\Delta(S)=\\{p\\in[0,1]^S:\\sum_i p_i=1\\}$. A time-dependent law is a coordinatewise continuously differentiable map $p:[0,\\infty)\\to\\Delta(S)$, with a right derivative at $t=0$, that satisfies the continuous-time master equation $\\dot p_i(t)=\\sum_{j\\ne i}[p_j(t)q_{ji}-p_i(t)q_{ij}]$ for every $t\\ge0$. A strictly positive normalized vector $\\pi$ is a detailed-balance law when $\\pi_iq_{ij}=\\pi_jq_{ji}$ for every distinct pair. Its undirected rate graph has an edge $\\{i,j\\}$ exactly when $\\pi_iq_{ij}>0$; detailed balance makes this equivalent to $\\pi_jq_{ji}>0$.\n\nOn a finite nonempty state set $S$, a discrete transition kernel is a matrix $K:S\\times S\\to[0,1]$ satisfying $\\sum_{j\\in S}K(i,j)=1$ for every $i\\in S$. For a strictly positive row probability vector $\\pi\\in\\Delta(S)$, the kernel satisfies $\\pi$-detailed balance when $\\pi_iK(i,j)=\\pi_jK(j,i)$ for every $i,j\\in S$. This identity pairs the supports of forward and reversed transitions. A rate model or a reversible kernel is a mathematical/physical model class; no observed trajectory is asserted by the definition.\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, $\\log$ is differentiable and\n$$\\log'(x)=\\frac1x,\\qquad \\log x=\\int_1^x\\frac{dt}{t}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A, B \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with\n$g[A] \\subseteq B$ and let $f : B \\to \\mathbb{R}$, so that the composite\n$f \\circ g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined. Let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of\n$A$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]) at which $g$ is differentiable\n([[def-derivative]]), put $b := g(c)$, and suppose $b$ is a limit point of $B$\nat which $f$ is differentiable. Then $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $c$ and\n\n$$(f \\circ g)'(c) \\;=\\; f'\\bigl(g(c)\\bigr)\\,g'(c) .$$\n\n**Both limit-point hypotheses are needed, and neither is automatic.** That $c$\nis a limit point of $A$ is what makes $g'(c)$ and $(f \\circ g)'(c)$ defined\nsymbols; that $b = g(c)$ is a limit point of $B$ is what makes $f'(b)$ one.\nNothing forces the second: $g$ may be differentiable at $c$ and send $c$ to an\nisolated point of $B$, and there $f'(b)$ is not defined and the formula asserts\nnothing.\n\n**No case analysis appears anywhere.** The naive difference-quotient proof\nwrites $\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(c))}{g(x) - g(c)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(c)}{x - c}$\nand then has to say what happens where $g(x) = g(c)$, which may occur at points\narbitrarily close to $c$. Carathéodory's factorisation never divides by the\ninner increment, so the difficulty does not arise.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of $A$\n([[def-limit-point-r]]), let $f, g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable at $c$\n([[def-derivative]]) and let $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then:\n\n1. $f + g$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(f+g)'(c) = f'(c) + g'(c)$;\n2. $\\alpha f$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(\\alpha f)'(c) = \\alpha f'(c)$;\n3. $fg$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(fg)'(c) = f'(c)g(c) + f(c)g'(c)$;\n4. if $g(c) \\ne 0$ then, writing $A_0 := \\{\\, x \\in A : g(x) \\ne 0 \\,\\}$, the\n   point $c$ lies in $A_0$ and is a limit point of $A_0$, the quotient\n   $(f/g)|_{A_0} : A_0 \\to \\mathbb{R}$, $x \\mapsto f(x)/g(x)$, is differentiable\n   at $c$ as a function on $A_0$, and\n   $$\\bigl((f/g)|_{A_0}\\bigr)'(c) \\;=\\; \\frac{f'(c)\\,g(c) - f(c)\\,g'(c)}{g(c)^{2}} .$$\n\nEach claim asserts two things: that the derivative on the left exists, and that\nit has the stated value. Both are proved.\n\n**Why claim 4 is stated on $A_0$.** The function $f/g$ is not defined where $g$\nvanishes, and $g$ may vanish at points of $A$ far from $c$; restricting to $A_0$\nis forced. That the restriction still has $c$ as a limit point, so that a\nderivative there means anything at all, is not free either, and it is the last\nclaim of [[lem-sign-preservation-near-a-limit]] applied to $g$. The hypothesis\nis $g(c) \\ne 0$, not \"$g$ vanishes nowhere\".\n\n**Everything is proved through\n[[thm-caratheodory-characterisation]].** No difference quotient is estimated and\nno limit theorem beyond continuity is used, so no choice principle is spent.\nThe four identities are four algebraic rearrangements of an increment, each\nfollowed by a reading of [[thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-derivative-of-exponential",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The real exponential function is $C^\\infty$, and for every $m\\in\\mathbb N$,\n$$\\exp^{(m)}=\\exp.$$\nIn particular $(\\exp)'=\\exp$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "cor-mean-value-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$ and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be\ncontinuous on $[a,b]$ ([[def-continuity-real]], [[def-interval]]) and\ndifferentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as a function on $[a,b]$\n([[def-derivative]]). Then there is $c \\in (a,b)$ with\n\n$$f(b) - f(a) \\;=\\; f'(c)\\,(b - a) .$$\n\nEquivalently, since $b - a \\ne 0$, there is $c \\in (a,b)$ at which\n$f'(c) = \\bigl(f(b)-f(a)\\bigr)/(b-a)$: the derivative somewhere inside equals\nthe average rate of change across the whole interval.\n\n**Continuity on the closed interval cannot be dropped.** Differentiability at\nevery point of $(a,b)$ alone does not suffice: a function on $[0,1]$,\ndifferentiable at every point of $(0,1)$ with derivative constantly $1$, for\nwhich no $c$ works, is exhibited later on this page as a false statement, and\nthe companion page works the same witness out in full.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ and let $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$, with the Euclidean\ninner product and the Euclidean norm as in [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. Then:\n\n1. **Cauchy-Schwarz.**\n   $$\\lvert\\langle x,y\\rangle\\rvert \\;\\le\\; \\lVert x\\rVert_2\\,\\lVert y\\rVert_2 ,$$\n   with equality if and only if there is a pair $(\\lambda,\\mu) \\ne (0,0)$ of\n   reals with $\\lambda x_k = \\mu y_k$ for every $k < n$.\n2. **$\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$ is a norm on $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$**\n   ([[def-norm-and-normed-space]]), for **every** $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$; the metric\n   it induces is $d_2$ of [[lem-metrics-on-rn]] whenever $n \\ge 1$.\n3. **Parallelogram law.**\n   $$\\lVert x+y\\rVert_2^{2} + \\lVert x-y\\rVert_2^{2} \\;=\\; 2\\lVert x\\rVert_2^{2} + 2\\lVert y\\rVert_2^{2} .$$\n4. **Polarisation.**\n   $$\\langle x,y\\rangle \\;=\\; \\tfrac14\\Bigl(\\lVert x+y\\rVert_2^{2} - \\lVert x-y\\rVert_2^{2}\\Bigr),$$\n   so the inner product is recovered from the norm it induces.\n\n**Clause 1 is a citation, not a new proof.** The inequality and its equality case\nare the published [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-finite]], stated there for two lists of\nreals; all that happens below is that it is read in the vector notation of\n[[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. Re-proving it here would put two proofs of one\nstatement in the library.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Put $c=1+\\max_i\\sum_{j\\ne i}q_{ij}$ and $P=I+Q/c$, where $Q=(q_{ij})$. Then $P$ has nonnegative entries and row sums one. The series $p(t)=e^{-ct}\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty (ct)^n p(0)P^n/n!$ and its differentiated series converge uniformly on bounded time intervals; its coefficients are nonnegative and sum to one, so it is a probability vector, starts at $p(0)$, and differentiates to $\\dot p=pQ$. Its $n=0$ term gives $p_i(t)\\ge e^{-ct}p_i(0)>0$. For uniqueness, the difference $z$ of two solutions satisfies $z'=zQ$; with $C=\\max_i\\sum_j|q_{ij}|$ and $U(t)=\\int_0^t\\|z(s)\\|_1ds$, one has $U'(t)=\\|z(t)\\|_1\\le C U(t)$. Thus $(e^{-Ct}U(t))'\\le0$ and $U(0)=0\\le U(t)$ force $U=0$, hence $z=0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F5",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "With $r_i=p_i/\\pi_i$, detailed balance gives $\\dot p_i=\\sum_{j\\ne i}a_{ij}(r_j-r_i)$. By [F4], differentiating the finite entropy sum gives $D'=\\sum_i\\dot p_i(\\log r_i+1)=\\sum_i\\dot p_i\\log r_i$, since $\\sum_i\\dot p_i=0$. Pairing the $i,j$ terms gives $D'=-\\sum_{\\{i,j\\}\\in E}a_{ij}(r_i-r_j)(\\log r_i-\\log r_j)\\le0$: if $r_i\\ge r_j$, then $\\log r_i-\\log r_j=-\\log(r_j/r_i)\\ge0$ by [F3], and the reversed order gives the same product sign. The mean value theorem [F5] on each compact time interval makes $D$ nonincreasing.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "Set $u_i=r_i-1$, so $\\sum_i\\pi_i u_i=0$, and $V(t)=\\sum_i\\pi_i u_i^2$. Since $\\dot u_i=\\pi_i^{-1}\\sum_{j\\ne i}a_{ij}(u_j-u_i)$, differentiating and pairing the two orientations of each edge gives $V'=2\\sum_i u_i\\sum_{j\\ne i}a_{ij}(u_j-u_i)=-2\\sum_{\\{i,j\\}\\in E}a_{ij}(u_i-u_j)^2$.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Let $a_*=\\min_{\\{i,j\\}\\in E}a_{ij}>0$. Fix a root state and choose one simple path from it to each state; this is only a finite family of choices, obtainable successively, and each path has length at most $N-1$. Telescoping and [F6] give $(u_i-u_r)^2\\le(N-1)\\sum_{\\{j,k\\}\\in E}(u_j-u_k)^2$. The zero-mean identity gives $u_r=-\\sum_i\\pi_i(u_i-u_r)$; expanding $\\sum_i\\pi_i[(u_i-u_r)-\\sum_j\\pi_j(u_j-u_r)]^2\\ge0$ yields $u_r^2\\le\\sum_i\\pi_i(u_i-u_r)^2$. Since $(x+y)^2\\le2x^2+2y^2$ and $\\sum_i\\pi_i=1$, it follows that $V\\le4(N-1)\\sum_E(u_i-u_j)^2\\le4(N-1)a_*^{-1}\\sum_Ea_{ij}(u_i-u_j)^2$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F6",
            "step 2.2",
            "algebra",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Thus $V'\\le-2\\lambda V$ with $\\lambda=a_*/[4(N-1)]>0$. By [F5], the derivative of $e^{2\\lambda t}V(t)$ is nonpositive and that function is nonincreasing, so $V(t)\\le e^{-2\\lambda t}V(0)$. Since $\\pi_i u_i(t)^2\\le V(t)$ and $p_i-\\pi_i=\\pi_i u_i$, each $p_i(t)\\to\\pi_i$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "step 2.2",
            "step 3.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
