      "finite_checks": [],
      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "alpha-batch-19",
        "notes": "Independently checked steps 1.1–4.1: finite stochastic P gives uniformly convergent Poisson series and positive n=0 term; integrating the difference gives uniqueness without an ODE assumption. Positive ratios make logarithmic derivatives valid, paired edges give Dprime <=0. Finite root paths and Cauchy–Schwarz give V <=4(N-1)/a_* times edge energy; Vprime=-2 energy gives exponential convergence. N>=2 and connectedness ensure a_*>0; p(0)>0 excludes log-zero. Fixed finite paths require no AC. This is a conditional rate-model deduction, with no data premise."
      }
    },
    "pthm-td-finite-jarzynski": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-td-driven-finite-path-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Take $\\Omega$ to be a finite nonempty microstate set as in [[def-td-finite-microstate-model]]: distinct elements count as distinct microstates, and the physical energy values are finite and measured in joules in the declared rest frame. For the physical internal-energy interpretation, use one declared fixed Galilean chart for every stage, with the bulk center-of-mass and separately tracked external-energy sectors suppressed from this finite protocol. The companion mechanical model supplies the center-of-mass data required by the microstate supplier; when the common chart is called a center-of-mass rest chart throughout the protocol, its center-of-mass velocity is constant over the modeled interval, and one constant boost selects that chart. This fixed-chart idealization does not infer a rest chart from the finite set or transition kernels, and does not describe an accelerating bulk body. Fix an integer $m\\ge1$ and stage energy functions $E_k:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R\\,\\mathrm J$ for $k=0,\\ldots,m$, with the energy offsets fixed by the declared protocol model. Fix inverse temperature $\\beta>0$ in joules$^{-1}$. The finite partition sum and canonical probability vector at stage $k$ are $Z_k=\\sum_{i\\in\\Omega}e^{-\\beta E_k(i)}$ and $\\pi_k(i)=e^{-\\beta E_k(i)}/Z_k$. Since $\\Omega$ is nonempty and finite and each energy is finite, $0<Z_k<\\infty$ and $\\pi_k$ is a strictly positive probability vector.\n\nA transition kernel at stage $k$ is a matrix $K_k(i,j)\\ge0$ with $\\sum_{j\\in\\Omega}K_k(i,j)=1$. In the forward stage order, the energy changes instantaneously from $E_{k-1}$ to $E_k$ while the state is still $i_{k-1}$; the subsequent bath transition samples $i_k$ using $K_k(i_{k-1},i_k)$ at fixed energy $E_k$. A path is $\\omega=(i_0,\\ldots,i_m)\\in\\Omega^{m+1}$. The initial law is a probability vector $\\mu_0:\\Omega\\to[0,1]$ with $\\sum_{i\\in\\Omega}\\mu_0(i)=1$. Its path probability is $P_F(\\omega)=\\mu_0(i_0)\\prod_{k=1}^mK_k(i_{k-1},i_k)$; summing over all paths gives $1$ by the normalization of $\\mu_0$ and the row sums of the kernels.\n\nWork on the system is positive when energy is supplied to it, and heat into the system is positive when a bath transition raises its energy. Define $W(\\omega)=\\sum_{k=1}^m[E_k(i_{k-1})-E_{k-1}(i_{k-1})]$ and $Q_{\\mathrm{in}}(\\omega)=\\sum_{k=1}^m[E_k(i_k)-E_k(i_{k-1})]$, both in joules. Define $F_k=-\\beta^{-1}\\log Z_k$ and $\\Delta F=F_m-F_0=-\\beta^{-1}\\log(Z_m/Z_0)$. These are finite-model quantities; their physical interpretation is conditional on the adopted preparation and transition assumptions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "post-td-canonical-protocol-preparation",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "For a protocol of [[def-td-driven-finite-path-model]], fix a bath temperature $T_b>0$ kelvin and set $\\beta=(k_{\\mathrm B}T_b)^{-1}$ in joules$^{-1}$, using the exact constant and SI conventions of [[def-td-si-quantities]]. The finite-state clause of the adopted [[post-td-canonical-sampling]] then gives the stage-zero law $\\pi_0(i)=e^{-\\beta E_0(i)}/Z_0$; adopt that the initial microstate is drawn from this law, so $\\mu_0=\\pi_0$. Here $\\Omega$ is finite and nonempty and $E_0(i)$ is finite, so $0<Z_0<\\infty$ as required by that clause. This is a model premise about the preparation distribution. It does not follow from the finite partition-sum definition, does not assert that an arbitrary system equilibrates in finite time, and does not report a measured preparation. The countable-state and measure-integral branches of [[post-td-canonical-sampling]] are not used.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "post-td-stationary-bath-kernel",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "For the protocol in [[def-td-driven-finite-path-model]], adopt, at each stage $k=1,\\ldots,m$, the stationarity condition $\\sum_{i\\in\\Omega}\\pi_k(i)K_k(i,j)=\\pi_k(j)$ for every $j\\in\\Omega$. This says that if the bath transition begins with law $\\pi_k$ at fixed energy $E_k$, it ends with the same law. It is a physical modeling assumption for this protocol family, not a consequence of the transition-kernel definition and not an assertion that the kernel mixes every initial law to $\\pi_k$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "post-td-canonical-sampling",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Assign a positive temperature $T$ in kelvins and the exact framework constant $k_{\\mathrm B}$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$ from [[def-td-si-quantities]], and put $\\beta=(k_{\\mathrm B}T)^{-1}$ in $\\mathrm{J^{-1}}$. For a finite microstate model $(\\Omega,\\epsilon)$, adopt the canonical preparation\n$$P_\\beta(x)=\\frac{e^{-\\beta E(x)}}{Z(\\beta)},\\qquad Z(\\beta)=\\sum_{x\\in\\Omega}e^{-\\beta E(x)}.$$\nHere $Z$ is dimensionless, positive, and finite because $\\Omega$ is finite and nonempty.\n\nThe same adopted law may be assigned to a specified countable state model with its stated enumeration and energy list, or to a measurable model $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu,E)$ with a dimensionless reference measure $\\mu$ and measurable energy $E:X\\to\\mathbb R\\,\\mathrm J$, only when $0<Z(\\beta)<\\infty$ for the declared series or integral. In those cases $P_\\beta(i)=e^{-\\beta E_i}/Z$ or the probability density is $p_\\beta=e^{-\\beta E}/Z$ relative to $\\mu$. The countable and integral conventions are those of [[def-tdmath-countable-canonical-model]] and [[def-tdmath-measure-integral]] at their actual current draft statements.\n\nThis is an adopted statistical-mechanical preparation rule. It is not derived from the macroscopic thermodynamic laws, a finite bath, entropy maximization, or a time-evolution equation. It does not assert that a physical sample has this preparation; that identification is an additional model assumption to be tested against a specified preparation and apparatus.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For $k\\ge1$ and $j\\in\\Omega$, define $S_k(j)$ by the finite sum over $i_0,\\ldots,i_{k-1}\\in\\Omega$ of $\\pi_0(i_0)\\prod_{\\ell=1}^kK_\\ell(i_{\\ell-1},i_\\ell)e^{-\\beta W_k}$, with $i_k=j$ and $W_k=\\sum_{\\ell=1}^k[E_\\ell(i_{\\ell-1})-E_{\\ell-1}(i_{\\ell-1})]$; put $S_0(j)=\\pi_0(j)$. Partitioning this finite sum by the state $i=i_{k-1}$ gives $S_k(j)=\\sum_iS_{k-1}(i)e^{-\\beta(E_k(i)-E_{k-1}(i))}K_k(i,j)$. The canonical preparation gives the base $S_0(j)=\\pi_0(j)=(Z_0/Z_0)\\pi_0(j)$ by [A1] and the finite branch of [L1].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "A1",
            "L1",
            "given",
            "base",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For the induction step fix $k\\in\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$ and suppose $S_{k-1}(i)=(Z_{k-1}/Z_0)\\pi_{k-1}(i)$ for every $i$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "ih",
            "step 1.1",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Substitute this hypothesis in the recurrence from step 1.1. Since $\\pi_{k-1}(i)=e^{-\\beta E_{k-1}(i)}/Z_{k-1}$, the previous-stage Boltzmann factor cancels against the quench factor, giving $S_k(j)=(Z_k/Z_0)\\sum_i\\pi_k(i)K_k(i,j)=(Z_k/Z_0)\\pi_k(j)$ by [A2]. This proves the induction step at each stage.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "A2",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "The base and induction step establish $S_k(j)=(Z_k/Z_0)\\pi_k(j)$ for all stages. At $k=m$, summing over terminal states and using normalization of $\\pi_m$ gives $\\mathbb E_F[e^{-\\beta W}]=Z_m/Z_0$. By $\\Delta F=-\\beta^{-1}\\log(Z_m/Z_0)$, this equals $e^{-\\beta\\Delta F}$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra",
            "discharge-induction"
          ]
        }
      ],
