    "pthm-td-ornstein-uhlenbeck-thermal-fluctuations": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "post-td-linear-ornstein-uhlenbeck-bath",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Choose a Galilean inertial chart $\\mathcal F$ as defined in [[def-td-galilean-rest-frame]] for the specified fixed closed trap-and-bath body, assumed to have positive total mass and the center-of-mass map required there, and supply its componentwise differentiable center-of-mass trajectory on the whole modeled time interval. Assume that its center-of-mass velocity is one constant vector on that interval, so the single constant boost of that supplier selects $\\mathcal F$ with zero center-of-mass velocity throughout. Take $\\mathcal F$ to be this common rest chart. The trap-and-bath background is held fixed in this idealization; center-of-mass recoil or accelerating-frame dynamics are not part of the scalar process. In this chart let $\\mathbf r_c\\in\\mathbb R^3$ be the fixed trap-center position and let $\\mathbf e\\in\\mathbb R^3$ be a fixed unit vector along the modeled trap axis. For a particle position $\\mathbf r\\in\\mathbb R^3$, define the signed displacement $x=\\langle\\mathbf r-\\mathbf r_c,\\mathbf e\\rangle$ in metres; the process $X_t$ in [[def-tdmath-ornstein-uhlenbeck-equation]] is this coordinate of the particle position at time $t$. Thus $U(x)=kx^2/2$ is the harmonic potential relative to the trap center, not an absolute spatial coordinate. Using the SI conventions and exact $k_{\\mathrm B}$ of [[def-td-si-quantities]], take stiffness $k>0$ in joules per square metre, constant mobility $\\mu>0$ in square metres per joule-second, and bath temperature $T_b>0$ kelvin. Adopt the one-dimensional overdamped model $dX_t=-\\gamma X_t\\,dt+\\sigma\\,dB_t$, interpreted through the integral equation of [[def-tdmath-ornstein-uhlenbeck-equation]], with $\\gamma=\\mu k$ in seconds$^{-1}$ and $\\sigma^2=2\\mu k_{\\mathrm B}T_b$ in square metres per second. Here $B$ is standard Brownian motion with its stated filtration and increment hypotheses, and the bath noise is independent of the initial preparation. This Einstein noise relation and the linear Markov model are physical assumptions. They do not follow from equilibrium thermodynamics alone and do not report a measured spectrum or calibration.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-ornstein-uhlenbeck-model",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice [[def-axiom-of-choice]]. Assume the filtered probability space, Brownian increment hypothesis, and initial data of [[def-tdmath-ornstein-uhlenbeck-equation]], including $\\mathbb E X_0^2<\\infty$ and future increments independent of $\\mathcal F_0$. For $\\gamma,\\sigma>0$, on every finite time interval the process\n$$X_t=e^{-\\gamma t}X_0+\\sigma e^{-\\gamma t}\\int_0^t e^{\\gamma s}\\,dB_s$$\nhas a continuous adapted version, solves the stated Ornstein–Uhlenbeck integral equation, and is the unique continuous solution driven by the same $(X_0,B)$. Put $V=\\sigma^2/(2\\gamma)$. For $0\\le s<t$, its transition law conditional on $\\mathcal F_s$ is normal with mean $e^{-\\gamma(t-s)}X_s$ and variance $V(1-e^{-2\\gamma(t-s)})$. Consequently\n$$\\mathbb E X_t=e^{-\\gamma t}\\mathbb E X_0,\\qquad\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)=e^{-2\\gamma t}\\operatorname{Var}(X_0)+V(1-e^{-2\\gamma t}).$$\nFrom any such initial law, $X_t$ converges in distribution to $N(0,V)$. If $X_0\\sim N(0,V)$ and is independent of future Brownian increments, the process is stationary Gaussian with covariance $C(\\tau)=Ve^{-\\gamma|\\tau|}$, and its transition kernel satisfies detailed balance with the $N(0,V)$ density. Its transition density solves the Fokker–Planck equation for positive time. Under the convention $S(\\omega)=(2\\pi)^{-1}\\int_{\\mathbb R}C(\\tau)e^{i\\omega\\tau}\\,d\\tau$, the stationary spectrum and time-average bound are\n$$S(\\omega)=\\frac{V\\gamma}{\\pi(\\gamma^2+\\omega^2)},\\qquad\\operatorname{Var}\\!\\left(\\frac1T\\int_0^T X_t\\,dt\\right)\\le\\frac{2V}{\\gamma T}.$$\nAll these statements concern the specified linear stochastic model. An Einstein relation between $\\sigma$ and a physical bath temperature would be an additional physical postulate.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-ornstein-uhlenbeck-equation",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,(\\mathcal F_t)_{t\\ge0},P)$ carry a standard Brownian motion $B$ adapted to the filtration, with future increments independent of the present sigma-algebra. Fix constants $\\gamma>0$ and $\\sigma>0$, and an $\\mathcal F_0$-measurable real random variable $X_0$ with finite second moment. A **continuous adapted solution of the Ornstein–Uhlenbeck equation** on $[0,T]$ is a continuous adapted process $X$ satisfying, almost surely for every $t\\in[0,T]$,\n$$X_t=X_0-\\gamma\\int_0^tX_s\\,ds+\\sigma B_t,$$\nwhere the time integral is the ordinary Lebesgue integral along the continuous path. In differential notation this is $dX_t=-\\gamma X_t\\,dt+\\sigma\\,dB_t$, with the stochastic differential interpreted through the displayed integral equation.\n\nThe definition asserts neither existence nor uniqueness. The Itô integral in the explicit representation is the square-integrable predictable-process integral of [[def-ito-integral-for-square-integrable-predictable-processes]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The physical postulate gives $V=\\sigma^2/(2\\gamma)=(2\\mu k_{\\mathrm B}T_b)/(2\\mu k)=k_{\\mathrm B}T_b/k$. This has units of square metres because $k_{\\mathrm B}T_b$ is joules and $k$ is joules per square metre.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The inspected mathematical supplier's stationary clause applies with this $V$ and $\\gamma=\\mu k$, so $C(\\tau)=Ve^{-\\mu k|\\tau|}$ and $S(\\omega)=V\\mu k/[\\pi((\\mu k)^2+\\omega^2)]=\\mu k_{\\mathrm B}T_b/[\\pi((\\mu k)^2+\\omega^2)]$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The same supplier's time-average bound becomes $2V/(\\gamma T)=2k_{\\mathrm B}T_b/(\\mu k^2T)$. All three formulas are conditional deductions of the adopted model; no apparatus, observed value, or measured uncertainty is asserted.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "This item has no empty-object boundary beyond the stated nonempty or normalized model inputs; no empty-model extension is claimed. Item: pthm-td-ornstein-uhlenbeck-thermal-fluctuations."
