    "pthm-td-qualified-landauer-reset": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-td-finite-memory-reset-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a finite memory-state set and $R$ a finite retained-register set. A joint preparation is a probability law $p(m,r)\\ge0$ with $\\sum_{m,r}p(m,r)=1$. Write $a_r=\\sum_m p(m,r)$ and, for $a_r>0$, $q(m|r)=p(m,r)/a_r$. Use the finite Shannon entropy in nats and the conditional entropy $H(M|R)$ exactly as defined by [[lem-tdmath-conditional-memory-entropy]]. For a fixed target memory state $m_0$, a reset is the endpoint map $p'(m,r)=\\mathbf 1_{\\{m=m_0\\}}a_r$: it places the memory at $m_0$ while leaving the register marginal unchanged. The definition alone assigns no physical entropy, work cost, heat transfer, or implementation.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-conditional-memory-entropy",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $M$ and $R$ take values in finite sets under a joint probability law $p(m,r)\\ge0$ with $\\sum_{m,r}p(m,r)=1$. For any finite random variable $Y$ with law $p_Y$, define its Shannon entropy by $H(Y):=-\\sum_{y:p_Y(y)>0}p_Y(y)\\log p_Y(y)$; for a pair, $H(M,R)$ means this definition applied to its joint law. Put $a_r:=\\sum_m p(m,r)$ and, when $a_r>0$, $q(m|r):=p(m,r)/a_r$. Thus $H(R)=-\\sum_{r:a_r>0}a_r\\log a_r$. Define $H(M|R):=\\sum_{r:a_r>0}a_r[-\\sum_{m:q(m|r)>0}q(m|r)\\log q(m|r)]$. Then $H(M,R)=H(R)+H(M|R)$ and $H(M|R)\\ge0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Initially the memory-register entropy is $k_{\\mathrm B}[H(R)+H(M|R)]$ by [F2] and [A1]. After reset, the memory is deterministic conditional on each register value, so $H(M'|R)=0$; applying the same finite entropy chain rule gives final entropy $k_{\\mathrm B}H(R)$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Therefore $\\Delta S_{\\mathrm{memory+register}}=-k_{\\mathrm B}H(M|R)$. Substitution into [A4] gives $Q_b\\ge k_{\\mathrm B}T_bH(M|R)$ because $T_b>0$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "A4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "By the adopted endpoint first-law convention [A3], $W=Q_b$, so $W\\ge k_{\\mathrm B}T_bH(M|R)$. The conclusion is conditional on [A1]--[A4]; the entropy bound alone does not assert that the reset can be implemented reversibly or that every bit erasure costs $k_{\\mathrm B}T_b\\log2$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "A3",
            "A4",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement assumes a normalized finite joint memory-register law, so an empty support cannot occur."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.1 allows H(M|R)=0; the resulting conclusion is W≥0 under the same physical assumptions."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement allows a singleton memory or register; the conditional entropy contribution is zero."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement includes the case where the memory is already at m_0 conditional on R; the reset map is unchanged and the bound reduces to W≥0."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement identifies entropy, energy, heat, and work at both reset endpoints."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "For the reset model of [[def-td-finite-memory-reset-model]], assume (i) the memory-register thermodynamic entropy is $k_{\\mathrm B}H(M,R)$ at both endpoints, (ii) a single ideal reservoir remains at fixed $T_b>0$ kelvin and receives heat $Q_b$, (iii) the memory-register endpoint energy is unchanged so the adopted first-law convention gives work on the system $W=Q_b$, and (iv) the total entropy change obeys $\\Delta S_{\\mathrm{memory+register}}+Q_b/T_b\\ge0$. Boundary nonempty-choice: No choice principle is used; the finite data are fixed and all sums are finite. Item: pthm-td-qualified-landauer-reset.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "For the reset model of [[def-td-finite-memory-reset-model]], assume (i) the memory-register thermodynamic entropy is $k_{\\mathrm B}H(M,R)$ at both endpoints, (ii) a single ideal reservoir remains at fixed $T_b>0$ kelvin and receives heat $Q_b$, (iii) the memory-register endpoint energy is unchanged so the adopted first-law convention gives work on the system $W=Q_b$, and (iv) the total entropy change obeys $\\Delta S_{\\mathrm{memory+register}}+Q_b/T_b\\ge0$. Then $W\\ge k_{\\mathrm B}T_bH(M|R)$. Equality is not asserted without a separately specified reversible implementation.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "e5821f05d0fc2c76e7a38a4be77a12a7639dba1d9fba0562094a82b02bae4334",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
