    "ex-td-biased-memory-reset-bound": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-td-finite-memory-reset-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a finite memory-state set and $R$ a finite retained-register set. A joint preparation is a probability law $p(m,r)\\ge0$ with $\\sum_{m,r}p(m,r)=1$. Write $a_r=\\sum_m p(m,r)$ and, for $a_r>0$, $q(m|r)=p(m,r)/a_r$. Use the finite Shannon entropy in nats and the conditional entropy $H(M|R)$ exactly as defined by [[lem-tdmath-conditional-memory-entropy]]. For a fixed target memory state $m_0$, a reset is the endpoint map $p'(m,r)=\\mathbf 1_{\\{m=m_0\\}}a_r$: it places the memory at $m_0$ while leaving the register marginal unchanged. The definition alone assigns no physical entropy, work cost, heat transfer, or implementation.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "pthm-td-qualified-landauer-reset",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For the reset model of [[def-td-finite-memory-reset-model]], use the exact SI value and conventions of [[def-td-si-quantities]]. Assume (i) the dimensionless Shannon entropies in nats from [[lem-tdmath-conditional-memory-entropy]] give memory-register thermodynamic entropy $S_{\\mathrm{memory+register}}=k_{\\mathrm B}H(M,R)$ in joules per kelvin at both endpoints, with $k_{\\mathrm B}$ in joules per kelvin, (ii) a single ideal reservoir remains at fixed temperature $T_b>0$ kelvin and receives heat $Q_b$ in joules, (iii) the memory-register endpoint energy is unchanged so the adopted first-law convention gives work on the system $W=Q_b$ in joules, and (iv) the total entropy change obeys $\\Delta S_{\\mathrm{memory+register}}+Q_b/T_b\\ge0$ in joules per kelvin. Then $W\\ge k_{\\mathrm B}T_bH(M|R)$, with both sides in joules. Equality is not asserted without a separately specified reversible implementation.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Substitution into the conditional reset theorem gives $H(M|R)=H(M)=-p\\log p-(1-p)\\log(1-p)$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Therefore the theorem gives $W\\ge k_{\\mathrm B}T_b[-p\\log p-(1-p)\\log(1-p)]$ for this preparation. It reports no work measurement and makes no claim about an apparatus.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra",
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
