    "ex-td-correlated-memory-zero-conditional-reset-cost": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-conditional-memory-entropy",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $M$ and $R$ take values in finite sets under a joint probability law $p(m,r)\\ge0$ with $\\sum_{m,r}p(m,r)=1$. For any finite random variable $Y$ with law $p_Y$, define its Shannon entropy by $H(Y):=-\\sum_{y:p_Y(y)>0}p_Y(y)\\log p_Y(y)$; for a pair, $H(M,R)$ means this definition applied to its joint law. Put $a_r:=\\sum_m p(m,r)$ and, when $a_r>0$, $q(m|r):=p(m,r)/a_r$. Thus $H(R)=-\\sum_{r:a_r>0}a_r\\log a_r$. Define $H(M|R):=\\sum_{r:a_r>0}a_r[-\\sum_{m:q(m|r)>0}q(m|r)\\log q(m|r)]$. Then $H(M,R)=H(R)+H(M|R)$ and $H(M|R)\\ge0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "pthm-td-qualified-landauer-reset",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For the reset model of [[def-td-finite-memory-reset-model]], use the exact SI value and conventions of [[def-td-si-quantities]]. Assume (i) the dimensionless Shannon entropies in nats from [[lem-tdmath-conditional-memory-entropy]] give memory-register thermodynamic entropy $S_{\\mathrm{memory+register}}=k_{\\mathrm B}H(M,R)$ in joules per kelvin at both endpoints, with $k_{\\mathrm B}$ in joules per kelvin, (ii) a single ideal reservoir remains at fixed temperature $T_b>0$ kelvin and receives heat $Q_b$ in joules, (iii) the memory-register endpoint energy is unchanged so the adopted first-law convention gives work on the system $W=Q_b$ in joules, and (iv) the total entropy change obeys $\\Delta S_{\\mathrm{memory+register}}+Q_b/T_b\\ge0$ in joules per kelvin. Then $W\\ge k_{\\mathrm B}T_bH(M|R)$, with both sides in joules. Equality is not asserted without a separately specified reversible implementation.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The memory marginal is uniform, so $H(M)=\\log2$. Given either value of $R$, the conditional law of $M$ is a point mass; hence each conditional entropy is zero and $H(M|R)=0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Therefore the theorem yields only $W\\ge0$ for this reset under its stated thermodynamic assumptions, while the unconditioned memory entropy is $\\log2$. It does not imply a $k_{\\mathrm B}T_b\\log2$ lower bound for this correlated preparation.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra",
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement fixes a normalized two-outcome joint law for the binary memory and register."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.1 computes H(M|R)=0 exactly, and step 2.1 records the resulting W≥0 bound."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Take memory and register bits $M,R\\in\\{0,1\\}$ with joint law $p(0,0)=p(1,1)=1/2$ and all other probabilities zero. Boundary one: This item is about a scalar continuous process or the explicitly fixed multistate example; no one-state or one-stage extension is claimed. Item: ex-td-correlated-memory-zero-conditional-reset-cost.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Example",
            "source_excerpt": "Take memory and register bits $M,R\\in\\{0,1\\}$ with joint law $p(0,0)=p(1,1)=1/2$ and all other probabilities zero. Retain $R$ and reset $M$ to 0.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "744a6b8a22f1a7bf1b2af6122f7d3a8e0633c03807e18735d04e4dfa1a771fbc",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
